+UNREVIEWED, UNLAUNCHED saved scalar diagnostic. No actual pivot coefficient replay or native Gram calls. This diagnoses the specific outward intervalC algorithm, not the true physical coefficient's intrinsic uncertainty.
+
+Let a newly selected half seed introduce a raw coordinate absent from the union of ALL previous raw supports (including Gamma partners). Its beta entry remains exactly+1/2 or−1/2: every earlier beta/Gamma beta is supported in the old union, so the recurrence cannot alter this new coordinate. An original insertion introduces exactly+1. If a half seed reuses raw support, this argument does not apply, even if its half label is new.
+
+For positive saved pivot interval[L,U], exact positive coefficient magnitude for a half is1/(2sqrt(r)). The image interval has width
+
+ w=1/(2sqrt(L))−1/(2sqrt(U))
+  =(U−L)/(2sqrt(L)sqrt(U)(sqrt(U)+sqrt(L)))
+  >=(U−L)/(4 U sqrt(U)).
+
+Thus any rational B>=sqrt(U) gives rigorous lower w>=(U−L)/(4UB). Insertion magnitude1 doubles this width. If this lower bound exceeds2^-39, the retained code's normalized width gate cannot pass at this row. A previous row might already stop it; this proves inability to complete through the offending row, not the exact first failure without full coefficient processing.
+
+An even more decisive scalar replay matches the code's primitive arithmetic without importing it. For saved integer endpoints l,u at S=2^192, sqrt interval is a=floor sqrt(l S), b=ceil sqrt(u S). For positive half coefficient, normalized integer bounds are floor(S²/(2b)) and ceil(S²/(2a)). Negative half has reversed signs and exactly the same width. For insertion remove2. These endpoints are forced because no previous-support term contributes; zero additions do not change them. If a=0, the existing positive interval divisor gate fails instead. This requires only isqrt and integer division on savedr, not full beta/C, old entries, roots/oracles or native arithmetic.
+
+The prospective checker scans exact saved selected indices and their canonical raw supports, records every newly introduced raw coordinate and the forced scalar width. It does not use relative-width decimal metadata as proof. It binds original four and12/24 source/acceptance lineage through the24 root closure and every actual24 history hash. All24 rows/fiveorbits are covered; step indices are zero-based. The first offending fresh row is an upper bound on where completion becomes impossible, not proof earlier reused-support rows pass.
+
+If confirmed, merely increasing coefficient working precision cannot fix the exact-image lower bound while the same savedr intervals are used. Narrower physical input/residual certificates, dependency-aware normalization that avoids treating the saved r interval independently, or a different representation not requiring these inverse coefficients are distinct possible routes. This is not a no-go for the exact subspace or leakage, and not permission to relax the fixed threshold or silently rerun anything.
